\description{
The corpus counterpart to \code{\link[=gr_fields]{gr_fields()}}: applies one extraction schema to
every document and returns a tidy table, one row per document and one column
per field, with a separate long table saying where every value came from.
}
\section{NA means two different things}{

A cell is \code{NA} either because the document was looked at and does not report
that field, or because the document was never successfully read. The \code{status}
column tells them apart, and it matters: "not reported" is a finding you can
publish, and "failed" is a job you have to redo. Filtering an extraction table
without checking \code{status} silently turns the second into the first.
}

\section{What it costs}{

One call per chunk per document -- every chunk is read, because a schema field
can be answered by a sentence anywhere in the paper and a retrieval step that
looked at the top eight chunks would miss it silently. Reconciliation is free
unless a document contradicts itself. Cut the cost by segmenting more coarsely
(\verb{max_tokens =}), not by looking at fewer chunks.
}

\section{Verifying it}{

Every filled cell is asked for the sentence it came from, and that sentence is
checked against the text of the chunk it was attributed to. Nothing is ever
discarded for failing: a paraphrase stays in \verb{$evidence} with
\code{verified = FALSE} and the fraction of it that did match in \code{match}.

\code{n_unverified} counts the cells in that row whose value could not be tied to a
verbatim span -- either because no quote was given, or because the quote is
not in the chunk. That column is the one to look at before believing a table:
\code{n_unverified} of zero means every value in the row can be pointed at in the
document. A row where it is not zero is not wrong, but it is unaudited, and
the answer is marked \code{partial} to say so. \code{require_quote = TRUE} turns the
count into a policy and drops those values instead.

See \code{\link[=gr_verify_evidence]{gr_verify_evidence()}} for the same check on a single answer.
}

\section{What to cite}{

In descending order of how long it stays true: the \code{quote}, which is verbatim
text and findable in the document whatever anyone changes; \code{page} and
\code{section}, which come from the document itself and are resolved per span, not
per chunk, so a quote is credited to the page it is on; \code{document_id}, the
hash of the cleaned text, which is the same in every run and on every machine.

\code{chunk_id} is none of those. It numbers the pieces the document was cut into
for \emph{this} segmentation, so re-running with a different \code{max_tokens} makes
chunk 7 a different piece of text. It is a pointer inside one run -- useful
for going back to \verb{$answers} -- and not a reference to publish.

There is deliberately no attempt to guess a document's title or authors from
its filename or its first few lines. A heuristic like that fails silently on
preprints, reports and anything scanned, and a citation that is wrong without
saying so is worse than none. Ask for them the same way as everything else --
\code{gr_fields(title = , authors = , year = , doi = )} -- and each comes back with
the sentence it was taken from and a check that the sentence is really there.
}

